msgid "We're almost done. In order to alleviate us from the burdens of formally manipulating easy to verify algebraic statements like the current goal, we've made a custom tactic ...\n"
"### **❯ The §0 tactic**\n"
"The §1 tactic will attempt to clear the goal by applying *very* basic simplification rules to the goal. For example, if the goal is §2, then §3 will clear it. 👉 In our case, try:\n"
"§4\n"
""
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L03_equiv
msgid "Currying is *extensively* used throughout Lean. We'll point out why when the time comes."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.PfWorld_eg.L07_pf
msgid "### **Level 7**"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L10_pf
msgid "Antisymmetric biconditional"
msgstr ""

#. §0: $A$
#. §1: $A$
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.SetWorld_eg.L07_set
msgid "The symmetric difference between §0 and the empty set is equal to §1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `hAB`
#. §1: ```
#. obtain h := hAB ha
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L01_set
msgid "See how the hypothesis §0 is now rewritten according to the definition? 👉 Now try\n"
"§1\n"
""
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L05_set
msgid "Set Difference"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rw`
#. §1: `⋂ i, A i`
#. §2: `⋂₀ F`
#. §3: `F`
#. §4: `∀`
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L08_set
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L10_set
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The §0 step was **necessary** here. This is because Lean sees §1 as being definitionally equivalent to §2 for an appropriate family §3. So we're once removed from the §4 statement we might expect."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `hk`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L05_pf
msgid "***Perfect.*** Let's rewrite the left-hand side of our goal with §0."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `⊢ A ⊆ B`
#. §1: `intro a ha`
#. §2: `intro a ha`
#. §3: $a$
#. §4: $A$
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L02_set
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"If we have a goal like §0, then the first line of our proof is (almost surely) §1.\n"
"\n"
"**🌐 How to translate.** In plain English, we translate a line like §2 into something like\n"
"> *Let §3 be an arbitrary element of §4.*"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `«{hxa}» : «{x}» ∈ A \\ B`
#. §1: `h`
#. §2: `«{x}» ∈ A ∪ C`
#. §3: ```
#. obtain hxAC : «{x}» ∈ A ∪ C
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L11_pf
msgid "Notice our assumption §0 is now in our proof-state, what's more, we have two goals. Let's make a third goal! Given §1 it seems like it would be good to obtain §2. 👉 So let's try\n"
"§3\n"
""
msgstr ""

#. §0: `exact subset_univ (⋃₀ (EquivClassFamily R))`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L15_EquivRelations
msgid "Every set is a subset of the universe, so this is trivial & Lean knows it - 👉 §0 closes the goal."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P`
#. §1: `a`
#. §2: `u`
#. §3: `x`
#. §4: `u`
#. §5: `P x`
#. §6: `exist`
#. §7: `exist`
#. §8: ```
#. exist a
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.L03_quant
msgid "## **Level 3**\n"
"This valid argument is sometimes called **Existential Generalization**. It says that if you know that a predicate (like §0) is true for some §1 of type §2, then we can deduce that there exists an element §3 of type §4 such that §5 is true. In order to prove this valid argument, we need a way to reckon with an existential goal in Lean. We need a new tactic ...\n"
"### **❯ The §6 tactic**\n"
"The most direct way to prove an existential goal is to supply a **witness** to the statement. The §7 tactic is handy in this regard. Specifically, 👉 try:\n"
"§8"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `obtain`
#. §1: `h2 : 16 * n = 80 * j`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L10_pf
msgid "***Super.*** Now, using the same ideas, §0 the hypothesis §1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `⊢ ¬¬isEven x → ¬¬isEven (x ^ 2)`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L03_pf
msgid "Notice that our goal is now §0. After getting rid of the double negations, we can approach this directly. See if you can't complete this level on your own from here."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `P`
#. §1: `R`
#. §2: `Q`
#. §3: `R`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L01_equiv
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The result of this level tells you that if you want to prove and if-then statement where the hypothesis is a disjunction, then you may do so by... **assuming** that §0 is true and showing that §1 must follow, ***and*** as a separate goal assuming that §2 is true and showing that §3 must following in this case as well."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Rel.pairs (Rel_id u)`
#. §1: `intro x hx`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L05_Relations
msgid "We'll walk through the first inclusion: it's a bit tricky! 👉 Grab an arbitrary element of §0 - §1."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ P ∧ ¬ P ↔ F₀ $$
#. §1: $F₀$
#. §2: $$
#. \\begin{array}{|cc||c|}
#.   \\hline
#.   P & ¬ P & P ∧ ¬ P \\
#.   \\hline
#.   T & F & F \\
#.   F & T & F \\
#.   \\hline
#. \\end{array}
#. $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.equiv_eg_docs
msgid "This is an **Inverse Law**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0\n"
"\n"
"where §1 is a contradiction. We can prove the **Inverse Law** with a truth-table\n"
"§2"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L01_pf
msgid "Definition unravelling"
msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ P ∨ F₀ ↔ P $$
#. §1: $F₀$
#. §2: ```
#. Or_false (P : Prop) : P ∨ False ↔ P
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.ArgWorld_eg.arg_eg_docs
msgid "This is an **Identity Law**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0\n"
"\n"
"where §1 is a contradiction. Here's what it looks like in Lean:\n"
"§2"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `{«{a}»,«{b}»} ⊆ A`
#. §1: `h`
#. §2: `{«{a}»,«{b}»} ∈ 𝒫 (A ∪ B)`
#. §3: `obtain`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L12_pf
msgid "Where to go from here? The main idea is to try to prove that §0 via the hypothesis §1. As a first step, let's try to show §2. Use §3 to open a new subgoal where this is the target."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `NAND`
#. §1: `⊼`
#. §2: ```
#. nand_def (P Q : Prop) : P ⊼ Q ↔ ¬ (P ∧ Q)
#. ```
#. §3: `⊼`
#. §4: `\\barwedge`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L06_equiv
msgid "# **Level 6: Not and Nand**\n"
"We introduce a (seemingly) new connective in this level: the §0 connective (not-and) denoted §1. Here's how it is defined in Lean:\n"
"§2\n"
"### **⌨ Typesetting-tip**\n"
"In order to typeset the symbol §3 type §4."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L13_pf
msgid "***Perfect.*** See if you can't finish this level on your own."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∨`
#. §1: `«{hxAC}» : «{x}» ∈ B ∪ C`
#. §2: `obtain`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L11_pf
msgid "We have another §0 hypothesis, namely §1. Use §2 to split our proof into cases."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `C`
#. §1: `H`
#. §2: `J`
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.ArgWorld_eg.L05_arg
msgid "# **Level 5**\n"
"Consider the following argument:\n"
"* Either Colonel Tigh is a cylon, or he's doing his job and the crew hates him.\n"
"* If Colonel Tigh is a cylon, then the crew hates him.\n"
"* Therefore, even if Colonel Tigh doesn't do his job, then the crew is still going to hate him.\n"
"\n"
"Let's validate this argument. We use:\n"
"* §0 : Colonel Tigh is a cylon\n"
"* §1 : The crew hates Colonel Tigh\n"
"* §2 : Colonel Tigh is doing his job\n"
">\n"
"See how the statement of this level is precisely this argument?"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `intro`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L09_pf
msgid "***Perfect.*** We now need to prove the **backward** implication. Let's §0 all the things."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∃`
#. §1: `∀`
#. §2: `∀`
#. §3: `∧`
#. §4: `Not_exists`
#. §5: `Not_exists`
#. §6: ```
#. rw[Not_forall] at h
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.L06_quant
msgid "# **Level 6**\n"
"A negated §0-statement turns into an §1-statement and vice versa. This follows (essentially) from DeMorgan's law (remember: §2 statements are like gigantic §3-statements). The theorem §4 codifies this relationship for us; 🔍 check out the entry for §5 in the **Theorems** tab. 👉 For our purposes here, try\n"
"§6"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `Not_not`
#. §1: `Not_not`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.L02_equiv
msgid "Here's where our newly unlocked theorem §0 can be handy. 🔍 Check out the entry for §1 in the **Theorems** tab."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∨`
#. §1: `⊼`
#. §2: `¬`
#. §3: `∨`
#. §4: `∧`
#. §5: `⊼`
#. §6: `True`
#. §7: `False`
#. §8: `⊼`
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L08_equiv
msgid "### **💡 All the food for thought**\n"
"The binary operator §0 can be expressed in terms of §1. The last three examples show that the unary operator §2 and the binary operators §3 and §4 can be expressed in terms of §5. In fact, *any* binary operator on §6 and §7 can be expressed in terms of §8!"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `R`
#. §1: `u`
#. §2: `isInfimum R l B`
#. §3: `l`
#. §4: `B`
#. §5: `u`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.Relation_docs
msgid "If §0 is a relation on §1, §2 means that §3 is the greatest lower bound for the subset §4 of §5."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `intro`
#. §1: ```
#. intro «{h}» «{A}» «{hA}» «{B}» «{hB}» «{x}» «{hx}»
#. ```
#. §2: `«{h}»`
#. §3: `x ∈ ⋃₀ F ∩ ⋃₀ G`
#. §4: `apply`
#. §5: `⊢ Q`
#. §6: `h : P → Q`
#. §7: `apply h`
#. §8: `⊢ P`
#. §9: ```
#. apply «{h}»
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L09_pf
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"We could have §0ed all these items in one go with\n"
"§1\n"
"Though that can be hard to see without the benefit of hindsight.\n"
">\n"
"Now, in light of assumption §2, we realize at this point that to prove our goal, it **suffices** to prove that §3. In order to implement this strategy we use...\n"
"### **❯ The §4 tactic**\n"
"If our goal is of the form §5 and we have a hypothesis of the form §6, then §7 will transform the goal into §8. 👉 In our case, try\n"
"§9\n"
""
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.FunctionWorld
msgid "Function World"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L15_EquivRelations
msgid "One more property..."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `a ∈ A ∩ B`
#. §1: `False`
#. §2: `h : A ∩ B = ∅`
#. §3: `obtain`
#. §4: `a ∈ B`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L04_pf
msgid "***Great.*** We're aiming to show that §0 -- but this is §1 since we know §2. To get there, let's §3 the fact that §4."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.QuantifierWorld_eg.L07_quant
msgid "# **Level 7**\n"
"Yet another practice problem."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `apply Double_inclusion`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.RelationWorldExamples.cartprod_example1
msgid "Double inclusion exercise involving Cartesian products.\n"
"\n"
"Note: It is possible to prove this just using rewrites, but you can also use this to get double inclusion practice. 👉 Start with §0 to set up your double inclusion proof."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L03_set
msgid "Set Intersection"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `R`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.Relation_docs
msgid "Makes a relation §0 an equivalence relation."
msgstr ""

#. §0: ```
#. (h₁ ∧ h₂ ∧ ⋯ ∧ hₙ) → c
#. ```
#. §1: `hᵢ`
#. §2: `c`
#. §3: `c`
#. §4: `hᵢ`
#. §5: `h₁ : P → Q`
#. §6: `h₂ : P`
#. §7: `h₁ (h₂)`
#. §8: `Q`
#. §9: `h₁`
#. §10: `P`
#. §11: `P`
#. §12: `Q`
#. §13: `Q`
#. §14: `rw`
#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld
msgid "# **Arguments**\n"
"An **argument** is an implication of the form\n"
"§0\n"
"The propositions §1 are called **assumptions** (or **hypotheses**), and the proposition §2 is called the **conclusion**. We say that an argument is **valid** to mean that §3 is true whenever the hypotheses §4 are all true. We say that an argument is **invalid** (or a **fallacy**) to mean that it is not valid. In terms of truth tables, an argument is valid means that any row where all the hypotheses are true will also report that the conclusion is true. The graphic in this world illustrates the valid argument known as *Modus Tolens*.\n"
">\n"
"Much like logical equivalences, the game is aware of some well-known valid arguments.But unlike logical equivalences, these well-known valid arguments are more baked into the *syntax* of Lean itself. For example, suppose we know §5 and §6. Then §7 is a proof of §8 -- it's as if §9 is a function whose input is §10 (or rather a proof of §11) and whose output is §12 (or rather a proof of §13).\n"
">\n"
"Whereas the proofs we wrote in Logical Equivalence World all relied on substitution (or the §14 tactic), our proofs in Argument World will begin to get more delicate. Let's get started!"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `f : Rel u v`
#. §1: `V : Set v`
#. §2: `v`
#. §3: `invimage f V`
#. §4: `u`
#. §5: `f(u) ∈ V`
#. §6: `f`
#. §7: `invimage f V`
#. §8: `u ∈ U`
#. §9: `f u v`
#. §10: `v ∈ V`
#. §11: `v`
#. §12: `f(u)`
#: Game.Levels.FunctionWorld.Function_docs
msgid "If §0 is a function and §1 is a set of elements of §2, then §3 is the set of elements of §4 with the property that §5.\n"
"\n"
"Since we can't directly \"plug in\" stuff to §6, the set §7 is defined as the set of §8 with the property that §9 for some §10. Of course, we know there is only one such possible §11, the one we usually call §12."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $i ∈ I$
#. §1: $A_i$
#. §2: $B_i$
#. §3: $A_i \\setminus B_i$
#. §4: $A_i$
#. §5: $B_i$
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.SetWorld_eg.L08_set
msgid "Suppose two families of sets are indexed over §0, say §1 and §2. The intersection of the §3 is equal to the intersection of all the §4 less the union of all the §5."
msgstr ""

#. §0: $$ ((P ∧ Q) → R) ↔ (P → (Q → R)) $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.equiv_docs
msgid "This is known as **Currying**. It says that\n"
"\n"
"§0"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L13_EquivRelations
msgid "With these two first useful lemmas out of the way, we're onto the more difficult stuff!"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.ArgWorld_eg
msgid "Argument World Examples"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `∨`
#. §1: $$ (P ∨ Q) ∨ R ↔ P ∨ (Q ∨ R) $$
#. §2: `Or_assoc`
#. §3: $$
#. \\begin{array}{|ccc||cc|}
#.   \\hline
#.   P & Q & R & (P ∨ Q) ∨ R & P ∨ (Q ∨ R) \\
#.   \\hline
#.   T & T & T & T & T \\
#.   T & T & F & T & T \\
#.   T & F & T & T & T \\
#.   T & F & F & T & T \\
#.   F & T & T & T & T \\
#.   F & T & F & T & T \\
#.   F & F & T & T & T \\
#.   F & F & F & F & F \\
#.   \\hline
#. \\end{array}
#. $$
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.equiv_docs
msgid "This is the **Associative Law** for §0. It says that\n"
"§1\n"
"The proof of §2 is given in the truth-table below:\n"
"§3"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `intro`
#. §1: `∧`
#. §2: `⊢ (P ∧ Q) → R`
#. §3: ```
#. intro ⟨hP,hQ⟩
#. ```
#. §4: `⊢ R`
#. §5: `hP : P`
#. §6: `hQ : Q`
#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.QuantifierWorld_eg.L05_quant
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"The §0 tactic can destructure §1 hypotheses as well. For example, if our goal is §2, then\n"
"§3\n"
"will turn the goal into §4 and introduce §5 and §6 as assumptions into the proof state."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rw[isEven] at hx hy`
#. §1: `isEven`
#. §2: ```
#. obtain ⟨k,hk⟩ := hx
#. obtain ⟨l,hl⟩ := hy
#. exist k+l
#. rw[hk,hl]
#. simplify
#. ```
#. §3: $x,y$
#. §4: $k$
#. §5: $2k = x$
#. §6: $l$
#. §7: $2l = y$
#. §8: `obtain ⟨k,hk⟩ := hx`
#. §9: `obtain ⟨l,hl⟩ := hy`
#. §10: $k+l$
#. §11: $x + y = 2(k+l)$
#. §12: `exist k + l`
#. §13: $2k + 2l = 2(k + l)$
#. §14: `rw[hk,hl]`
#. §15: `simplify`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L01_pf
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip:**\n"
"The first line §0 wasn't necessary. Lean knows that §1 is definitionally an existential statement. Here's the shorter version of this same proof:\n"
"§2\n"
"**🌐 How to translate.** Here's how to translate this Lean proof into a human-readable proof line-by-line.\n"
"> *Since §3 are even, let §4 be an integer such that §5 and let §6 be an integer such that §7.*\n"
"> These are the lines §8 and §9.\n"
">\n"
"> *Consider the integer §10. We aim to prove that §11.*\n"
">\n"
"> This is the line §12.\n"
">\n"
"> *By substitution, this is equivalent to showing §13.*\n"
">\n"
"> This is the line §14.\n"
">\n"
"> *After simplifying, this follows by reflexitivity.*\n"
">\n"
"> This is the line §15.\n"
">\n"
"There are smoother ways to write this proof, but this is the most logically direct translation from Lean to human-readable."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `R`
#. §1: `u`
#. §2: `isMinimal R b`
#. §3: `b`
#. §4: `u`
#. §5: `b`
#. §6: `B`
#. §7: `u`
#. §8: `isMinimal R b B`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.Relation_docs
msgid "If §0 is a relation on §1, §2 means that §3 is a minimal element of §4.\n"
"\n"
"If we want to specify that §5 is a minimal element of a subset §6 of §7, write §8."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `⊼`
#. §1: ```
#. nand_def (P Q : Prop) : P ⊼ Q ↔ ¬ (P ∧ Q)
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.equiv_eg_docs
msgid "The definition of the **Negated Conjunction** connective, denoted §0. Here's what it looks like in Lean:\n"
"§1"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.RelationWorldExamples.equivrelation_example1
msgid "If equivalence relations have the same family of equivalence classes, they're the same relation."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.L02_Cartesianproducts
msgid "## **Level 2: Cartesian products**\n"
"\n"
"The previous level suggests that we can conclude that an element fails to be in a Cartesian product as long as at least one coordinate fails to be in the required set; specifically, in terms of this statement, the first coordinate.\n"
"Let's prove it."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.RelationWorldExamples.relation_example2
msgid "This level gives some good practice with composite relations."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.RelationWorldExamples
#: Game.Levels.FunctionWorld.FunctionWorldExamples
msgid "### ***In-class examples and HW problems***"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `⊢ x ∈ A → x ∈ B`
#. §1: `⊢ x ∈ B → x ∈ A`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L06_pf
msgid "Our *Active Goal* is §0 while *Goal 2* is §1. See if you can't finish from here."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `I`
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L10_set
msgid "See how the goal is an existentially quantified statement over the indexing set §0 now? We need a witness."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `exist!`
#. §1: `∃! x, P x`
#. §2: `a : u`
#. §3: `exist! a`
#. §4: `⊢ P a`
#. §5: `⊢ ∀ y, P y → y = 0`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.pf_docs
msgid "The §0 tactic is helpful for existence/uniqueness goals. Specifically, if the goal is of the form §1 and §2 is a witness to goal, then §3 will split the goal into two subgoals. The first goal is §4 (the existence statement). The second goal is §5 (the uniqueness statement)."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `S`
#. §1: `u`
#. §2: `subsetOrder S`
#. §3: `S`
#. §4: `F`
#. §5: `⋂F`
#: Game.Levels.RelationWorld.RelationWorldExamples.orderrelation_example2
msgid "Recall that, if §0 is a subset of §1, §2 is the subset partial order on §3.\n"
"\n"
"In this exercise, you'll prove that the greatest lower bound of a family of sets §4 is §5."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `simplify`
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L05_pf
msgid "If only there were a tactic that could help us §0 basic arithmetic like in our current goal..."
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.EquivWorld.EquivWorld_eg.L05_equiv
msgid "More practice"
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L01_set
msgid "Subset Relation"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `a ∈ ⋂₀ F`
#. §1: `rw`
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.L07_set
msgid "### **💡 Pro-tip**\n"
"Lean knows that §0 is definitionally a universal statement, so the §1 step was unnecessary in this proof."
msgstr ""

#. §0: `⊢ False`
#. §1: `exact`
#. §2: `«{hAB}»`
#. §3: `contradiction`
#. §4: `False`
#. §5: `«{hAB}»`
#. §6: `False`
#. §7: `h : P`
#. §8: `h' : ¬ P`
#. §9: `contradiction`
#. §10: `False`
#. §11: `False → True`
#. §12: `True`
#. §13: ```
#. contradiction
#. ```
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.L04_pf
msgid "We can clear the goal in two different ways. Our goal is §0; this is §1ly what is being said in §2. Alternatively, we could use ...\n"
"### **❯ The §3 tactic**\n"
"One of our hypotheses definitionally says §4 (namely §5). Whenever we have ahypothesis that is explicitly §6, or we have a pair of hypotheses that are trivially contradictory (like suppose we have §7 and §8 -- these hypotheses are contradictory) the the §9 tactic will clear the goal. The idea being that under §10 assumptions, anything follows (remember: §11 is a §12 implication). Give it a try with\n"
"§13\n"
""
msgstr ""

#: Game.Levels.ArgWorld.ArgWorld_eg.L07_arg
#: Game.Levels.QuantifierWorld.QuantifierWorld_eg.L05_quant
#: Game.Levels.SetWorld.SetWorld_eg.L05_set
#: Game.Levels.PfWorld.PfWorld_eg.L05_pf
msgid "Example 5"
msgstr ""

#. §0: `rw`
#. §1: `image`
#. §2: `invimage`
#: Game.Levels.FunctionWorld.L04_Functions
